h2 { "Cooperative GPU kernels, GPU text and multi-backend bundles" }

Import std/gpu when individual GPU invocations need to coordinate over shared workgroup memory. dispatch launches fixed-size workgroups across rank zero through eight numeric output shapes on Metal, Vulkan and CUDA. WorkItem provides globalIndex and localIndex coordinates, fixed-size numeric scratch via item.shared[T](count) returning Shared[T], and workgroup synchronization via item.barrier(). The compiler proves barrier convergence across branches, helpers and bounded loops, verifies shared-memory initialization and disjoint lane writes, and checks device limits without falling back to CPU execution.

Functions spawned on Metal, Vulkan or CUDA now accept String values and read-only [String] batches (up to 16 KiB per row in cooperative kernels). Device code supports Unicode-scalar length, byteLength, scalar indexing, half-open slice, exact equality, concatenation, and variable-length owned String results returned to the host.

Linux and Windows releases now include combined multi-backend GPU compiler archives (kalandra-native-gpu-standalone-linux-x86_64.tar.gz and experimental Windows x86_64 and arm64 equivalents) alongside the single-backend cuda and vulkan archives. Compiling with --target cuda,vulkan produces one application directory with both PTX and SPIR-V artifacts. At runtime the first GPU request tries CUDA, then Vulkan, and caches the admitted device for the process. Set KALANDRA_GPU_BACKEND to force a backend or KALANDRA_GPU_TRACE=1 to inspect selection.

