h2 { "Streams, subprocesses, directory watching and TCP networking" }

table {
  row = ["Module or feature", "What is available"],
  row = ["std/stream", "ByteReader, ByteWriter and PipeCloser traits with generic readInto, readAppend, write, writeAll and close over ByteBuffer."],
  row = ["std/process", "command, working-directory and environment overrides, piped or inherited stdio, start, takeStdin/takeStdout/takeStderr, cancellable wait and bounded capture."],
  row = ["std/fs trees and watching", "copyTree, removeTree, symlink inspection/creation (symlinkMetadata, readLink, createSymlink) and native watchDir event iterators."],
  row = ["std/net", "listenTcp, localPort, accept, connectTcp, connectTcpWithin, shutdownWrite, per-endpoint setTimeout, and split into independent TcpReader and TcpWriter halves."]
}

Files, buffered readers/writers, subprocess pipes and TCP endpoints share the std/stream traits, so one generic byte pump works across all four. Bounded DNS resolution limits concurrent OS lookups and discards stale results on cancellation. Suspended accept, read and write operations release their worker thread and wake immediately on OS socket readiness (kqueue on macOS, epoll on Linux, WSAPoll on Windows).

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/net" import listenTcp, localPort, connectTcp, accept, shutdownWrite, NetError
from "std/stream" import readInto, writeAll, StreamError
from "std/buffer" import byteBuffer, append, BufferError
from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn main() -> unit ! (NetError | StreamError | BufferError | IoError):
  listener = try listenTcp("127.0.0.1", 0)
  client = try connectTcp("127.0.0.1", try localPort(listener))
  server = try accept(listener)

  chunk = try byteBuffer(64)
  try append(chunk, [75, 97, 108, 97, 110, 100, 114, 97])
  try writeAll(client, chunk)
  try shutdownWrite(client)

  received = try readInto(server, chunk, 64)
  try println(format("{}", received))
  return ()
""" }

This prints 8 after sending eight bytes over loopback and signaling EOF from the client half.

